{
  "ResearchDossier": {
    "topic": "how the barcode was invented",
    "summary": "The barcode began in 1948 when Bernard Silver overheard a supermarket president asking for automated checkout; Norman Woodland drew Morse-code dots and dashes in the sand of a Florida beach and stretched them into narrow and wide lines. Patented in 1952, it first scanned a product, a pack of Juicy Fruit gum, in Troy, Ohio in 1974.",
    "angle": "The lines on every package are Morse code someone dragged through beach sand.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "id": "c1",
        "text": "In 1948 Bernard Silver, a Drexel graduate student, overheard the president of the Food Fair supermarket chain asking a dean to research automatic checkout; Silver told his friend Norman Woodland.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barcode",
            "title": "Barcode - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "overheard the president of the local food chain, Food Fair, asking one of the deans to research a system"
          },
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norman_Joseph_Woodland",
            "title": "Norman Joseph Woodland - Wikipedia"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c2",
        "text": "At his grandfather's apartment in Florida, Woodland drew dots and dashes like Morse code in the beach sand.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norman_Joseph_Woodland",
            "title": "Norman Joseph Woodland - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "drew dots and dashes in the sand similar to the shapes used in Morse code"
          },
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barcode",
            "title": "Barcode - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "he formed his first barcode from sand on the beach"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c3",
        "text": "Woodland: 'I just extended the dots and dashes downwards and made narrow lines and wide lines out of them.'",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barcode",
            "title": "Barcode - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "I just extended the dots and dashes downwards and made narrow lines and wide lines out of them."
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c4",
        "text": "Woodland and Silver filed a patent on 20 October 1949; US Patent 2,612,994 'Classifying Apparatus and Method' was granted on 7 October 1952 and covered linear and circular bullseye designs.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norman_Joseph_Woodland",
            "title": "Norman Joseph Woodland - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "circular bulls-eye printing designs"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c5",
        "text": "On 26 June 1974 at 8:01 a.m., a 10-pack of Wrigley's Juicy Fruit gum was the first product scanned with a UPC, at the Marsh supermarket in Troy, Ohio; it rang up at 67 cents.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universal_Product_Code",
            "title": "Universal Product Code - Wikipedia"
          },
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barcode",
            "title": "Barcode - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "a 10-pack of Wrigley's Juicy Fruit gum was scanned, registering the first commercial use of the UPC"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      }
    ],
